{
  "transcript": "Here's what happens the second someone calls you something. You're cocky. You're intense. You're a player. Most guys do one of two things, get defensive or fire something mean back. Both lose. Defending says the label got to you. Firing back says you're rattled. There's a third move, and it's the whole exercise. Agree to the word, then redefine it. It works because the same fact can be said a dozen ways, none of them truer than the rest. Player and outgoing social guy mean the exact same thing, a guy who talks to a lot of people. One sounds like a warning. One sounds like a catch. You just chose the word. So when she hands you a label, you don't argue the fact. You swap the word for a better one. If by cocky, you mean confident, yeah, I've had a few wins. What can I say? But here's the part most people get wrong, and it's the entire point. You have to land on a clear positive. Someone calls you an asshole and you go, if by asshole you mean cocky, you went from one negative to a different negative. Better negative still a negative. Why stop it cocky when you can go to confident? Push it all the way. Confident, assertive, the guy who knows what he wants, not almost good. Good. And notice what you didn't do. You didn't get defensive. You didn't explain. You didn't get mean and turn it on her. You calmly took the word and made it mean something you'd be proud of. That's what reads as status. The dig landed and it didn't move you an inch. Keep it short too. Three clauses deep into your whole philosophy and it sounds try hard. A clean line lands. A paragraph dies. And the same move works before anyone hands you a label. You're choosing your own words all day. Sorry, could I get your number? Sounds like she's doing you a favor. You don't seem like bad company. I'll take your number makes the exact same ask sound casual, mutual, same fact, you just picked how it lands. This goes way past dating. A jab in a meeting, a knock in a review, someone calls you blunt. Instead of flinching, you relabel it on the spot. If by blunt, you mean I'll tell you the truth when it's expensive, and the room recalibrates who it's dealing with. So here's the drill. A few minutes a day, have someone throw a label at you and flip it. Take the word, find the version that's actually a compliment, and land on the positive. Keep at it, and it stops being something you reach for. Next time someone tries to knock you down a peg, you won't flinch. The better word just shows up already on your tongue. That's second nature. So put in the reps. That's how a cheap shot stops landing on you and starts making you look good.",
